# REST SCREEN

Version 1.0

---

# Purpose

The Rest Screen exists to maximize the quality of the next effort.

Rest is not inactivity.

Rest is preparation.

The athlete should recover physically while preparing mentally for the next set.

The screen should eliminate uncertainty.

---

# Core Principle

During rest, the athlete should always know:

- how much time remains;
- what comes next;
- what will be required;
- when to move.

Nothing else matters.

---

# Primary Question

The Rest Screen answers one question:

"When do I start again?"

---

# Information Hierarchy

The Rest Screen follows this order:

1. Remaining rest time
2. Next exercise
3. Next prescription
4. Previous set summary
5. Session progress
6. Secondary actions

---

# Countdown

The countdown is the dominant element.

It occupies the visual center of the screen.

Typography:

Display

Large.

Highly readable.

Visible from several meters away.

---

# Countdown Behaviour

The countdown updates every second.

Transitions remain smooth.

No blinking.

No flashing.

No jumping digits.

Time should feel continuous.

---

# Final Countdown

The last five seconds receive subtle emphasis.

Recommended sequence:

5

4

3

2

1

GO

The emphasis may include:

- subtle haptic feedback;
- optional voice guidance;
- slight typography emphasis.

Never flashing colors.

Never alarm-style behaviour.

---

# Rest Duration

The prescribed duration should remain visible.

Example:

Rest

02:00

The athlete immediately understands:

Current remaining time

↓

Original prescribed duration

---

# Next Exercise

The next exercise is always visible.

Recommended content:

Exercise name

Load

Repetitions

Tempo (if prescribed)

Round or set number

Example:

NEXT

Military Press

42.5 kg

6 reps

---

# Equipment Preparation

When useful, CAS reminds the athlete what equipment should already be prepared.

Examples:

Prepare:

• 42.5 kg

• Bench

• Resistance band

These reminders remain concise.

---

# Previous Set Summary

The athlete receives immediate confirmation.

Example:

Completed

80 kg × 5

RPE 8

Everything recorded successfully.

The athlete should never wonder whether the set was saved.

---

# Session Progress

A discreet progress indicator remains visible.

Example:

Session

58 %

The athlete knows how far they are from completing the workout.

---

# Voice Guidance

Optional.

Recommended announcements:

30 seconds remaining.

10 seconds remaining.

Next exercise.

Target load.

Target repetitions.

Begin.

Voice guidance remains concise.

No motivational speeches.

---

# Haptic Feedback

Recommended:

Last five seconds.

Rest complete.

Skipped rest.

Never use continuous vibration.

---

# Skip Rest

Skip Rest is available.

It remains visually secondary.

The athlete may resume immediately.

No confirmation dialog unless clinically necessary.

---

# Extend Rest

The athlete may extend rest.

Recommended increments:

+15 s

+30 s

+60 s

CAS records this behaviour for future coaching.

---

# Automatic Adaptation

Future versions may recommend longer or shorter rest based on:

Heart rate.

Velocity loss.

Fatigue.

RPE.

Readiness.

The recommendation should always explain why.

---

# Phone Lock

The countdown continues while the phone is locked.

Returning to the application always displays the correct remaining time.

No timer drift.

No restart.

---

# Background Execution

Rest timing survives:

Phone lock.

Incoming call.

Notification.

Temporary backgrounding.

The athlete never loses timing.

